eling, estimation and validation of cotton dust and endotoxin exposures in Chinese textile operations</article-title><source>Ann. Occup. Hyg</source><year>2006</year><volume>50</volume><fpage>573</fpage><lpage>582</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16632488</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b23-ijerph-06-00622"><label>23.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lavou&#x000e9;</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>B&#x000e9;gin</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Beaudry</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>G&#x000e9;rin</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Monte Carlo simulation to reconstruct formaldehyde exposure levels from summary parameters reported in the literature</article-title><source>Ann. Occup. Hyg</source><year>2007</year><volume>51</volume><fpage>161</fpage><lpage>172</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17046960</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="f1-ijerph-06-00622" position="float"><label>Figure 1.</label><caption><p>A Bayesian process chart.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ijerph-06-00622f1"/></fig><table-wrap id="t1-ijerph-06-00622" position="float"><label>Table 1.</label><caption><p>Parameters measured in the factory (90 %CI).</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Code</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Workstation</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Parameter</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Number of measurements</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>AM</bold> or <italic>GM</italic></th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Minimum</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Maximum</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="3" valign="top" colspan="1">1.1</td><td align="left" rowspan="3" valign="top" colspan="1">Slurry applying</td><td a